WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.240
Many cryptographic operations depend on values that are unpredictable, unique, or both.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:14.340
Keys, salts, nonces, initialization values, and session material may look like ordinary bytes, but their security requirements are specific.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:21.760
Using a general-purpose pseudo-random function for these values is not acceptable unless the library explicitly documents that use.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:26.140
Cryptographically secure random sources are designed for security-sensitive generation.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:34.520
In C applications, that usually means using the operating system, platform API, or cryptographic library function approved for this purpose.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:42.260
The code should handle random-generation failure as a real error, because continuing with weak or missing randomness can invalidate the design.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.520
Nonces and initialization values are not all secret.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:51.420
Many require uniqueness or unpredictability according to the primitive and mode being used.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:58.600
A nonce may be stored or transmitted with the protected data, but reuse can still be unsafe when the primitive requires uniqueness.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:02.660
Developers should follow the library's rules rather than relying on intuition.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:12.000
Startup assumptions matter. Embedded systems, early-boot services, containerized processes, and restricted environments may have different random-source behavior.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:19.180
The application should use approved APIs and should make startup failures visible when secure randomness is not available.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.400
Silent fallback to weaker generation is not a safe recovery strategy.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:30.580
When the library can manage nonces, initialization values, salts, or generated keys safely, prefer that path.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:36.560
Library-managed values reduce the chance of local mistakes and often encode the expected size and format.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:44.540
When the application must provide values, tests should verify size, uniqueness policy, storage behavior, and error handling.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:55.960
Reviewers should ask where every security-sensitive random or unique value comes from, how failure is handled, and whether the value is secret, unique, unpredictable, or simply a label.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:59.700
Clear answers prevent accidental misuse of strong primitives.
